<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Potsdam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1685</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> was issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, in October 1685 in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s revocation of the Edict of Nantes. It offered French Huguenot refugees safe settlement, tax exemptions and freedom of worship, and some 20,000 settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>, many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, bringing crafts and industries that strengthened the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam.jpg" alt="Edict of Potsdam" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Der Große Kurfürst Friedrich Wilhelm (The Great Elector) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EdiktPotsdam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
